A||N||14||Will||Engineers should not be allowed to design things! (rated R--under 17 not admitted)||||1||
Z||000000||Will||05-19-2001||11:11 PM||||I recenty made a set of delrin bushings for my car. I thought installing the lowers was going to be easier than installing the uppers. WRONG!<P>I had to unbolt the steering rack from the chassis to remove the front pivot bolts for the lower control arms. The is no room  to get a ratchet on the nut of the rear pivot bolt; which means the bolt gets turned by the ratchet; which means the situation gets worse, because the bolt is seized to the sleeve which it pulls loose from the rubber when turned.<P>None of that $*!* would happen if a mechanic had designed this car instead of an engineer.<P>There is no design consideration given to maintenance of the suspension! WTF?<P>The thing that <I><B>REALLY</B></I> gets my dandruf up is that the front and rear pivots for the lower control arm are parellel, but NOT CONCENTRIC.<P>WHAT IN THE F!@#$%^ WORLD WAS THE MOTHER F!@#$%^, CHOAD SMOKING IGNORAMUS WHO DESIGNED THE PIECE OF S&!@ i CALL MY CAR THINKING WHEN THAT ONE FELL OUT OF HIS @$$ AND INTO THE TOILET?????????<P>Grrrrrrrrrrrrrr....<P>That these pivots are not concentric F@#$% into a cocked hat any attempt to use anything but rubber bushings in this F@#$%^& suspension. When hard bushings, like delrin, are installed, now the control arm and/or chassis mounts have to FLEX when the suspension goes through its travel. It's not a problem with rubber bushings, because they deform so much. Thanks to some cheese dick engineer who couldn't tell the accountants to F@#$% off and let him design it right my car is screwed for the use of decent bushings!!!!!!!!!<P>OK, I'm done now.<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 05-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Pontiaddict||05-20-2001||12:28 AM||||It's a "Suspension by Opel thing" (If you are talking about a pre '88 design, which I think you are.)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||sjp777||05-20-2001||12:36 AM||||Ow, my ears.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_2.jpg">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||10
Z||000003||EdsB52||05-20-2001||02:08 AM||||Design engineers design devices for performance and manufacturing engineers design devices for ease of assembly. GM is not in the business of maintaining vehicles, just manufacturing them. If they can reduce any measureable amount of time from the manufacturing process, even at the cost of increased maintenance years down the road, they will in a second without doing an analysis of it. Do you think GM or Ford caters to people who buy 13 year old junk? Me either. They cater to people who buy new cars. They promote the idea of junking cars after 4 years and buying new. Why would they try to make it easier for guys like us? This phenomenon is a component of "planned obsolesence." If you want a horror story, hear about me spending 8 hours changing plugs and wires on a 94 Z28 Camaro. You have to do it from the bottom, remove both header pipes with the cat and the starter. You end up changing the #2 (r/h fwd) spark plug from the back of the engine while laying on the ground. I would charge $200 to do it if I owned a shop.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||05-20-2001||10:59 AM||||Will--Could you please rephrase the part about the babboon?  I don't care about the rest of it, but I'm kinda partial to primates.<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000005||lawrence||05-20-2001||06:46 PM||||I hear a lot of people complaining about engineers.  I can't say for sure in this case, but I can tell you that it's normally the Managers that force engineers to do things that they normally wouldn't do.<P>My guess is that the engineer would rather have desinged the car starting with a blank piece of paper.  But of course, that would be too expensive and take too long (all concerns of a Manager) so it wasn't done that way.<P>Sorry, had to speak my mind on this.<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/sts" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/sts</A>  - Sequential Turn Signals<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/products" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/products</A>  - WebElectric Products<p>[This message has been edited by lawrence (edited 05-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||EdsB52||05-20-2001||08:25 PM||||Exactly, that's what I was saying. Engineering decisions are generally cost driven, not intellectually driven. They give you enough performance to make you want to buy the product and maintenance isn't even in the equation. I used to beat my head against the wall asking myself what the rationale was for an engineer designing a part a certain way with inherent interferences. Now I realize he wasn't out to get me and my colleagues, he simply didn't care either way. Again, manufacturers try to appeal to consumers that buy new products, not the grease monkeys that have to mess with it 10+ years later. If they mention maintenance, it's that the vehicles have large maintenance intervals and don't need maintenance as often, as in the Cadillacs. How about Mitsubishi's eclipse. They advertise sexy styling with good-looking girls having fun, going out dancing, etc.  They might advertise 230 HP or something relating to cost per bang-for-the-buck, but never ease of maintenance.<P>I'm watching a Toyota commercial where young white-collar people are shown buying new cars while kissing. The announcer talks about sales events and, "right here. Right now." Nothing about ease of changing spark plugs.<P>In an indirect way, car manufacturers need the second car economy. But in another way they hate us. They need to keep their used cars with good resale, but they also need planned obsolescence and legislation in effect by having older cars junked so people buy new. There's a whole sociology behind new cars, and manufacturing and sales. But maintenance is most commonly used as a deterrent, deterring people from buying older cars, while bragging about the ease of certain cars maintenance. It's kind of like the police petitioning for more money to fight crime. They advertise high crime rates, hence the need for more police. Then next time around they need to show the benefit that the increased expenditures have yielded, while showing the need for more money. They can't show that crime has subsided, hence less money is needed. It's extremely paradoxical. Anyway, these are both dynamics that are double-edged, in that they refute the same thing they promote, just on a different day.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Ken Wittlief||05-21-2001||03:44 PM||||"I recenty made a set of delrin bushings for my car"<P>  I hate to say this, but if you are going to make improvements and modify your car, cause you think you can make it better than the engineers did who designed it, then you really cant complain if the car was not 'designed' to fit your modifications, or to be easy for you to modify.<P>  I dont think bushings were ever intended to be a normal replacement item - they were suppose to last the life of the car. I dont think you will find a maintance schedule for the fiero that says to replace the O2 sensor, transmission filter, and suspension bushings every 60,000 miles.<P> You gotta remember too the fiero is from the days of the Chevette, the 30,000 mile throwaway car.<P>  this is still happening today. i know a guy who had a geo metro and needed a new head after 30,000 miles (it was warped). He could not get one, anywhere. He could not get it from the factory. Nobody rebuilt them. Nothing: Ziltch.<P>  the car is in a junk yard now, and he is driving a honda civic.<P>  I hope this doesnt sound insulting or condesending. If you take on the challenge of modifying or restoring a car, it doesnt come across very well if you slam the engineers that created the car that you love so much that you want to restore/modify it.<P>  If the engineers were perfectionist then there would be nothing that would need modification, or restoration - and I probally couldnt afford to buy one if they were really outstanding machines.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 05-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||EdsB52||05-21-2001||08:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>"I recenty made a set of delrin bushings for my car"<P>  I hate to say this, but if you are going to make improvements and modify your car, cause you think you can make it better than the engineers did who designed it, then you really cant complain if the car was not 'designed' to fit your modifications, or to be easy for you to modify.<P>  I dont think bushings were ever intended to be a normal replacement item - they were suppose to last the life of the car. I dont think you will find a maintance schedule for the fiero that says to replace the O2 sensor, transmission filter, and suspension bushings every 60,000 miles.<P> You gotta remember too the fiero is from the days of the Chevette, the 30,000 mile throwaway car.<P>  this is still happening today. i know a guy who had a geo metro and needed a new head after 30,000 miles (it was warped). He could not get one, anywhere. He could not get it from the factory. Nobody rebuilt them. Nothing: Ziltch.<P>  the car is in a junk yard now, and he is driving a honda civic.<P>  I hope this doesnt sound insulting or condesending. If you take on the challenge of modifying or restoring a car, it doesnt come across very well if you slam the engineers that created the car that you love so much that you want to restore/modify it.<P>  If the engineers were perfectionist then there would be nothing that would need modification, or restoration - and I probally couldnt afford to buy one if they were really outstanding machines.<P>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 05-21-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>True dat||||reg||1
