Z||000010||Will||05-22-2001||08:14 AM||||I was venting my frustrations more than anything. I understand that the Fiero was crippled by bean counters from the day 1.<P>Now for the topic of easily accepting modifications:<P>That the pivots are not concentric is an engineering mistake. Since the chassis was originally designed for the '88 suspension, it had to be reworked for the chevette suspension anyway. Since they were in the process of reworking the frame, it would not have been difficult or expensive to move the rear pivot out the half inch it would have taken to make things concentric. And if the pivots were concentric, the rear bushing would undoubtedly last longer. Both inner sleeves in my rear bushings broke loose from the rubber quite easily, while the fronts are still reasonably well bonded.||||reg||1
Z||000011||lowCG||05-22-2001||10:13 PM||||So why did you choose Delrin(?),just because some Fiero speed shop uses it for suspensions doesn't mean that it'll last and won't compress.<BR>There's a compound called UHMW that'll work alot better for that application,and since you already know how much fun it can be replacing suspension parts...||||reg||1
Z||000012||Will||05-22-2001||10:33 PM||||I'm making UHMW bushings for the rear control arms.<P>UHMW is softer than delrin. I wanted to see how long it would last in bushings that are not as hard to replace as the fronts before I made a set for a whole car out of it.<P>I'm also more concerned about XL UHMW which is supposed to have better NVH characteristics than regular UHMW, although it's expen$ive.||||reg||1
Z||000013||terryk||05-22-2001||11:13 PM||||I've been saying that all along...||||reg||12
Z||000014||lowCG||05-23-2001||11:46 PM||||I had a set that I cobbled together on an old lathe in the rear of my old car;after a year I checked the play and there was none.<BR>I ran the cutter(boring bar) back and forth,inside to make a relief for some teflon grease.I think I only had around .005-.010 of crush when I inserted the sleeve,and after a year of hard cornering,the action was bearing smooth with no slop.I sold the car after a year so I can't vouch for longetivity after that,and it was a four cylinder.<BR>I polished the sleeves real good with a Scotchbrite pad in the lathe also.||||reg||1
